#: ../effects.rst:1104
msgid "The **Denoise Image** effect reduces visible video noise, grain, and color speckles. It is useful for low-light camera footage, noisy webcam clips, high-ISO photos, old video, compressed footage, and rendered images that have unwanted speckling."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1108
msgid "For a quick start, drag **Denoise Image** onto a clip and preview the result. The default settings are designed to clean up common noise while keeping faces, text, edges, and fine details recognizable. If the image still looks too noisy, raise :guilabel:`Strength`. If the image starts to look too smooth, raise :guilabel:`Detail`."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1113
msgid "Simple controls"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1115
msgid ":guilabel:`Strength` controls how much denoising is applied."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1116
msgid ":guilabel:`Detail` protects edges and texture. Higher values keep more detail; lower values remove more speckles."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1117
msgid ":guilabel:`Color Noise` targets red, green, and blue color speckles, which are common in dark video."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1119
msgid "Most users can start with those three controls. The next controls are helpful when you want cleaner video without creating ghosting or smearing:"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1122
msgid ":guilabel:`Temporal` blends a small amount of information from the previous frame during normal playback. This can reduce dancing noise from frame to frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1124
msgid ":guilabel:`Motion Safety` protects moving objects. Higher values are more cautious and reduce the chance of ghosting."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1125
msgid ":guilabel:`Response Curve` changes denoise strength by brightness. By default, shadows are cleaned more strongly, midtones are balanced, and highlights are affected more lightly."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1129
msgid "How it works"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1131
msgid "Denoise Image combines **spatial denoising** and conservative **temporal denoising**. Spatial denoising looks at the current frame and smooths small noisy variations while trying to preserve important edges. Temporal denoising compares neighboring frames and only blends when the image appears stable enough. When OpenShot detects a seek, scrub, frame jump, or still image, temporal history is reset automatically, so the effect falls back to the current frame only."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1136
msgid "The effect also treats brightness and color differently. Dark areas usually contain more camera sensor noise, so the default response curve applies stronger cleanup in shadows. Bright areas usually need less denoising. Color speckles are reduced more aggressively than luma detail, which helps remove low-light chroma noise without turning the whole image into a blur."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1142
msgid "Usage tips"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1144
msgid "For mild camera noise, use the default settings or raise :guilabel:`Strength` slightly."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1145
msgid "For very noisy dark footage, raise :guilabel:`Strength` and :guilabel:`Color Noise`, then lower :guilabel:`Detail` only as much as needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1147
msgid "For faces, text, hair, leaves, or detailed textures, keep :guilabel:`Detail` higher to avoid a smeared look."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1148
msgid "For moving subjects, keep :guilabel:`Motion Safety` high and avoid pushing :guilabel:`Temporal` too far."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1149
msgid "For still images or photos, :guilabel:`Temporal` has no useful history, so focus on :guilabel:`Strength`, :guilabel:`Detail`, :guilabel:`Color Noise`, and :guilabel:`Response Curve`."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1158
#: ../effects.rst:1197
msgid "strength"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1158
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` Overall denoise amount. Higher values remove more grain and speckles."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1159
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` Edge and texture protection. Higher values preserve more fine detail; lower values smooth more noise."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1160
msgid "temporal"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1160
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` Previous-frame blending amount for stable sequential video frames."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1161
msgid "motion_safety"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1161
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` Motion protection. Higher values reduce temporal blending where movement is detected."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1162
msgid "color_noise"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1162
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` Extra cleanup for red, green, and blue color speckles."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1163
msgid "``(curve)`` Brightness-based denoise response. Use it to denoise shadows, midtones, and highlights differently."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1168
msgid "The Displacement Map effect uses a grayscale image or video to warp the current frame in the horizontal and vertical directions. This effect is also commonly called a **distortion map**, **warp map**, **displace effect**, or **displacement effect**. It is useful for heat haze, water ripples, refractive glass, mirage effects, animated distortion overlays, shockwave looks, and other stylized screen warps."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1173
msgid "The map source is sampled for every pixel. **Mid-gray** is neutral and causes no movement. **Darker** and **lighter** areas shift pixels in opposite directions. A still image creates a fixed distortion pattern, while a video or animated map creates moving distortion over time. Transparent parts of the map smoothly fade back toward neutral displacement."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1178
msgid "Usage Notes"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1180
msgid "Use a **grayscale image or grayscale video** for the most predictable results."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1181
msgid "Use **Map: Source** to pick an imported file, image sequence, video clip, or even a transition image as the map."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1182
msgid "**Strength** multiplies both the horizontal and vertical displacement."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1183
msgid "**Horizontal** controls left/right warping as a percentage of the frame width."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1184
msgid "**Vertical** controls up/down warping as a percentage of the frame height."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1185
msgid "**Brightness** and **Contrast** reshape the map before displacement, which can make the warp subtler or more dramatic."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1186
msgid "**Map: Invert** flips the direction driven by light and dark areas."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1187
msgid "**Replace Image** is a debug preview that shows the processed map instead of the warped frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1195
msgid "map_reader"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1195
msgid "``(reader)`` **Map: Source.** Select the grayscale image, animation, video, or transition file used as the displacement source"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1196
msgid "``(int, choices: ['Yes', 'No'])`` **Map: Invert.** Reverse the displacement direction driven by dark and light areas"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1197
msgid "``(float, 0 to 3)`` Overall multiplier for the displacement effect"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1198
msgid "horizontal"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1198
msgid "``(float, -1 to 1)`` Horizontal displacement amount, as a percentage of the frame width"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1199
msgid "vertical"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1199
msgid "``(float, -1 to 1)`` Vertical displacement amount, as a percentage of the frame height"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1200
msgid "``(float, -1 to 1)`` Brightness adjustment applied to the displacement map before warping"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1201
msgid "``(float, 0 to 20)`` Contrast adjustment applied to the displacement map before warping"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1202
msgid "``(int, choices: ['Yes', 'No'])`` Replace the output image with the processed map, useful for previewing or debugging the distortion map"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1207
msgid "The **Film Grain** effect adds a gentle moving texture to your video, similar to the tiny speckles you see in real film photography. This can make very clean digital footage feel warmer, more natural, or more cinematic. It can also help blend mixed footage together, especially when one clip looks too sharp or too smooth compared to the rest of your project."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1212
msgid "If you are new to film grain, start small. A little grain can add life and texture without calling attention to itself. Stronger settings can be useful for vintage looks, music videos, horror scenes, documentary recreations, or footage that should feel like older 16mm or Super 8 film."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1216
msgid "You can add Film Grain from the :guilabel:`Effects` tab, or right-click a clip and choose :guilabel:`Look → Film → Film Grain` to start with a preset: :guilabel:`35mm Fine`, :guilabel:`35mm Classic`, :guilabel:`35mm Gritty`, :guilabel:`16mm Classic`, :guilabel:`Super 8`, or :guilabel:`High ISO`. Presets only set the properties for you; all controls remain visible and editable."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1221
msgid "Simple starting points:"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1223
msgid "**Clean cinematic texture**: try :guilabel:`35mm Fine`, then lower ``amount`` if it feels too visible."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1224
msgid "**Classic film look**: try :guilabel:`35mm Classic` for a balanced grain pattern."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1225
msgid "**Punchy, gritty film look**: try :guilabel:`35mm Gritty` for heavier, more visible grain."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1226
msgid "**Older home-movie style**: try :guilabel:`Super 8`, which uses larger, more active grain."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1227
msgid "**Low-light camera noise style**: try :guilabel:`High ISO`, then adjust ``color_amount`` to control how colorful the grain feels."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1229
msgid "The grain is deterministic, which means the same settings render the same grain pattern each time. Change ``seed`` when you want a different repeatable grain pattern on a clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1238
#: ../effects.rst:1540
msgid "amount"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1238
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` Overall grain intensity. Lower values are subtle; higher values are more visible and gritty."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1239
#: ../effects.rst:1315
msgid "size"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1239
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` Grain scale. Lower values create fine grain; higher values create larger, coarser grain."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1240
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` Softens the grain texture. Lower values look crisp; higher values look smoother and more organic."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1241
msgid "clump"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1241
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` Controls how even or clustered the grain appears. Higher values create more irregular groups of grain."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1242
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` Grain strength in dark areas of the image."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1243
msgid "midtones"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1243
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` Grain strength in middle brightness areas, such as skin tones and everyday objects."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1244
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` Grain strength in bright areas, such as skies, windows, and lights."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1245
msgid "color_amount"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1245
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` How much the grain affects color. Lower values are mostly luma grain; higher values add more chroma grain."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1246
msgid "color_variation"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1246
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` How independently the red, green, and blue grain changes. Higher values feel more colorful and random."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1247
msgid "evolution"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1247
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` How much the grain renews over time. Higher values make the texture change more from frame to frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1248
msgid "coherence"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1248
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` How stable and smooth the grain remains between frames. Higher values feel calmer and less jumpy."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1249
msgid "seed"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1249
msgid "``(int, 0 to 1000000)`` Selects the exact repeatable grain pattern. Change this to get a different look without changing intensity."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1254
msgid "Grain is easiest to judge while the video is playing, not on a single paused frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1255
msgid "If faces or bright skies look too noisy, lower ``highlights`` or ``amount``."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1256
msgid "If shadows look too clean compared to the rest of the image, raise ``shadows`` slightly."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1257
msgid "If the grain looks too digital or sharp, raise ``softness`` or lower ``color_variation``."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1258
msgid "If the grain looks too busy during motion, lower ``evolution`` or raise ``coherence``."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1262
msgid "The Glow effect creates a soft halo from the clip's visible pixels. It can render either outside the subject for a classic outer glow, or along the inside edges for an inner glow. The effect uses the source alpha channel, so transparent PNGs, text, logos, and masked clips work especially well."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1266
msgid "You can add Glow from the :guilabel:`Effects` tab, or right-click a clip and choose :guilabel:`Look → Lighting → Glow` to start with a preset: :guilabel:`Soft White` (a gentle neutral halo), :guilabel:`Warm` (a warm amber glow), :guilabel:`Neon` (a vivid colored glow), or :guilabel:`Inner Glow` (glow drawn inside the subject's edges). Presets only set the properties for you; all controls remain visible and editable."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1278
#: ../effects.rst:1543
#: ../effects.rst:1694
msgid "mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1278
msgid "``(int, choices: ['Outer', 'Inner'])`` Choose whether the glow appears outside the visible pixels (Outer) or just inside their edges (Inner)."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1279
#: ../effects.rst:1565
msgid "opacity"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1279
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` Overall glow strength and transparency."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1280
#: ../effects.rst:1566
msgid "blur_radius"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1280
msgid "``(float, 0 to 100)`` Blur radius in pixels used to soften the glow. Larger values create a wider, softer halo."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1281
#: ../effects.rst:1316
#: ../effects.rst:1567
msgid "spread"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1281
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` Expands and strengthens the source alpha before blurring for a denser, more filled-in glow."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1282
msgid "``(color)`` Tint color of the glow, including alpha. Use the alpha channel to control the glow's maximum opacity independently of the ``opacity`` property."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1287
msgid "The Hue effect adjusts the overall color balance of the video, changing the hues without affecting the brightness or saturation. This can be used for color correction or to apply dramatic color effects that transform the mood of the footage."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1297
msgid "hue"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1297
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` The curve to adjust the percentage of hue shift"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1302
msgid "The Lens Flare effect simulates bright light hitting your camera lens, creating glowing halos, colored rings and gentle glares over your footage. Reflections are automatically placed along a line from the light source toward the center of the frame. You can animate any property with keyframes to follow your action or match your scene."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1312
msgid "``(float, -1 to 1)`` Horizontal position of the light source. -1 is left edge, 0 is center, +1 is right edge."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1313
msgid "``(float, -1 to 1)`` Vertical position of the light source. -1 is top edge, 0 is center, +1 is bottom edge."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1314
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` Overall glow strength and transparency. Higher values make brighter, more opaque flares."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1315
msgid "``(float, 0.1 to 3)`` Scale of the entire flare effect. Larger values enlarge halos, rings and glows."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1316
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` How far secondary reflections travel. 0 keeps them close to the source, 1 pushes them all the way toward the opposite edge."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1317
msgid "tint_color"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1317
msgid "``(color)`` Shifts the flare colors to match your scene. Use the RGBA sliders to pick hue and transparency."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1322
msgid "The Negative effect inverts the colors of the video, producing an image that resembles a photographic negative. This can be used for artistic effects, to create a surreal or otherworldly look, or to highlight specific elements within the frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1328
msgid "The Object Mask effect creates a detailed, animated mask around a subject that you identify. Instead of drawing a rectangular tracking box, you mark the subject with points or rectangles and OpenShot follows its visible outline through the clip. Use it to highlight a person or product, create a colored cutout or outline, or provide the :guilabel:`Mask: Source` for another effect such as Blur, Pixelate, or Color Grade."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1333
msgid "Object Mask runs locally in OpenShot and does not require a ComfyUI server. OpenShot uses an `EfficientSAM model <https://github.com/OpenShot/openshot-onnx/tree/main/efficient-sam>`_ to turn your prompts into a mask on selected frames, then uses `Cutie models <https://github.com/OpenShot/openshot-onnx/tree/main/cutie>`_ to propagate that mask through the video. These OpenCV-friendly model packages are maintained in the `OpenShot ONNX repository <https://github.com/OpenShot/openshot-onnx>`_. The model files are downloaded the first time you use the effect and are stored for later use."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1342
msgid "Creating an Object Mask"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1343
msgid "Drag :guilabel:`Object Mask` from the :guilabel:`Effects` panel onto a video clip."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1344
msgid "In the initialization dialog, choose a :guilabel:`Quality` level and download the Object Mask model files if they are not installed. Higher quality generally takes more processing time and memory."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1346
msgid "Leave :guilabel:`Processing Device` set to :guilabel:`CPU` for maximum compatibility, or select an available GPU option. :guilabel:`GPU (Auto)` falls back to CPU when a supported GPU backend is unavailable."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1348
msgid "Click :guilabel:`Select Points`. On a frame where the subject is clearly visible, add at least one positive point or rectangle on the subject. Add negative points or rectangles over nearby background or unwanted objects when they help separate the subject."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1351
msgid "Enable the mask preview to check the selected area. Add prompts on other frames if the subject changes greatly, becomes hidden, or the initial selection is ambiguous."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1353
msgid "Click :guilabel:`Process Effect`. Processing analyzes the clip and creates the animated mask data used by the effect. Cancel the job if you need to revise the prompts or settings."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1356
msgid "After processing, select the Object Mask effect to change its fill and outline. To apply another effect only inside the detected subject, add that effect to the same clip and select Object Mask under its :guilabel:`Mask: Source` property. Use :guilabel:`Mask: Invert` on the other effect when you want to modify the background instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1361
msgid "Object Mask and Advanced AI"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1362
msgid "Object Mask is the quickest built-in workflow when you need a reusable mask inside an OpenShot project. The similar ComfyUI :guilabel:`Mask...`, :guilabel:`Blur...`, and :guilabel:`Highlight...` workflows generate new media files through a separately installed ComfyUI server and provide more customizable AI pipelines. See :ref:`ai_tracking_ref` for those Advanced AI workflows."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1376
#: ../effects.rst:1445
msgid "draw_mask"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1376
msgid "``(int, choices: ['Yes', 'No'])`` Show or hide the colored mask overlay."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1377
#: ../effects.rst:1464
msgid "mask_color"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1377
msgid "``(color)`` Color drawn over the selected subject."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1378
#: ../effects.rst:1465
msgid "mask_alpha"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1378
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` Opacity of the colored mask overlay."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1379
msgid "stroke_color"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1379
msgid "``(color)`` Color of the outline around the selected subject."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1380
#: ../effects.rst:1460
#: ../effects.rst:1767
msgid "stroke_alpha"
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1380
msgid "``(float, 0 to 1)`` Opacity of the subject outline."
msgstr ""

#: ../effects.rst:1381
